term time is delayed for many school  children across england with the  education secretary telling us this  morning  that he is hopeful those primary schools  that do not have a return date at the  moment  will reopen i'm joined now by paul  whiteman general secretary of the  national association  of head teachers and paul thank you for  joining us in this morning  um when i was speaking to the education  secretary he  suggested that a week was enough time to  have a uh  set up a fully functioning testing  system and do you agree with him  that's not what the members that i  represent are telling me  um putting a testing center together  that requires seven distinct roles  uh for staff to undertake we're told  through the government's guidance  that it will take nine staff some three  hours to test 100 pupils  this is a huge undertaking and it's a  huge undertaking that directs attention  away from the education effort and what  we've been saying to government for some  time now  is if if education really is the  priority that you say it is  stop laying um duty upon duty upon duty  on schools let them get on with the  education effort and bring support in  uh to undertake these tasks we we accept  that mass testing could be a game  changer for schools  but we can't administer mass testing and  educate at the same time so we really  need some support  with all of that in mind and given the  fact that um so much of the country is  now  seeing tier four restrictions do you  think schools should reopen  not until we're ready to do so so we  were we were talking to government  uh about three weeks before the  christmas break three weeks before the  end of term  looking at the data looking at the  indications particularly around  secondary schools and saying then  make the decision now that there'll be a  later opening so that we can plan  during those weeks to have an orderly  return to school properly supported  and yet again the government makes the  decision at the very last minute when  there's no time to plan  and no time to react to that decision  and the decision that schools and local  authorities were trying to take on their  own accounts shortly before  uh christmas uh and then the government  threatened them with legal action for  trying to organize  uh a a an orderly uh orderly arrangement  for education  this polarized debate about school gates  are open and therefore education is  happening or school dates are closed and  education doesn't happen  it is misleading what school leaders  need is the flexibility to make good  education decisions about those children  that they teach at home those that can  be best taught at home  and those children that need to be in  front of a teacher face to face  uh to make sure that we can really  deliver education it's not just about  gates open and gates closed and this  fixation with the gates are open or  closed isn't helping us  with that education effort and the worry  about primary schools too  um you know the big debate has been  about the announcement over secondary  schools we've got about 15  of primary schools closed right now now  with this new variant we don't know what  direction that's taken and how  infectious  younger children are to to adults in the  community and to each other  and until we understand that we need to  take care with primary why should we  wait until we're another tipping point  with another  another area of the population before we  make good decisions  so we want children in school children  need to be in school but let's organize  an orderly  and orderly and safe return to school  rather than making a mess of it at every  turn  and on remote learning ahead of the  first national lockdown there was  a promise that those children who needed  access to the internet who needed  um laptops they would be delivered  um a lot of questions a lot of criticism  and that that  didn't materialize um are you confident  that  those children who will have to remote  learn will have access to  things like wi-fi and um and a laptop in  order to be able to study effectively  well not all of the kit has been  delivered yet and i noticed in the  secretary of state statement yesterday  the promise to deliver even more of the  million devices that were promised still  fell  short of that million promise so there  are more out there  but the members i represent tell me that  they're not arriving on time  and we have to also get away from just  talking about um  learning at home being through computers  not every child learns well through that  medium and what we've got to do is allow  school leaders and teachers the freedom  to build learning packages that really  deliver  for the children that they're teaching  so let's get the kids out there but  let's not be fixated just about kit  let's have a proper conversation  about what learning at home can look  like it will never replace face-to-face  teaching and we do need to get that  orderly return organized  but in the meantime while we while we  organize that orderly return  let's do the very best that we can get  the kit out there the government needs  to deliver it but let's not be fixated  on that  as the only delivery channel especially  younger children
 

